.TH PHASE-OMEGA 7 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
phase-omega \- COLD STEEL: stage 2 attack against vuln2(1)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The second of two phases. The target is
.BR vuln2 (1).

.TS
center, allbox;
l l.
defense	state
stack canary	off
NX (executable stack)	ON
PIE	off
ASLR (kernel)	on
.TE

The same source file as
.BR vuln1 (1),
linked without
.BR -z\ execstack
and built with
.B \-DLEAK_LIBC
so it also prints the runtime address of
.BR printf (3)
in libc. The stack is no longer executable. Your Phase Phi exploit,
even with the offset and address recomputed for this binary, will
fault on the first instruction of the shellcode.

.SS THE LESSON

NX (No-eXecute) defeats classical "shellcode on the stack" attacks.
You cannot run code from a writable page. You must instead make the
program return into something that
.B is
already executable. The standard answer is the C library, libc, which
is mapped into every process. Specifically: the
.BR system (3)
function takes one string argument and runs it as a shell command. If
you can return into
.B system
with the string
.B "/bin/sh"
on top of the stack as its argument, you have your shell.

.SS THE PAYLOAD SHAPE

The naive ret2libc payload is:

.nf
[ filler to offset ][ &system ][ &exit ][ &"/bin/sh" ]
.fi

For x86 32-bit cdecl, arguments are passed on the stack in order. The
.B &exit
slot is where
.B system
will return; we point it at
.BR exit (3)
so the program exits cleanly after the shell does.

This payload, however, is not enough by itself. See "setuid drop"
below.

.SS SETUID DROP

.B vuln2
is setuid
.BR oracle .
The kernel sets EUID = oracle, RUID = student when you run it. So
when
.B system("/bin/sh")
fires,
.BR dash (1)
starts with EUID = oracle and RUID = student. Linux dash treats this
as a privilege boundary it should not cross silently and drops EUID
back to RUID at startup. The shell that opens for you runs as
.B student
and cannot read
.I /srv/flag2.txt
(mode 0400, owner
.BR oracle ).
The kernel-level setuid drop also takes the saved-set-UID with it, so
there's no way back from inside the shell.

The fix is to make EUID == RUID before
.B system
fires, so dash has nothing to drop. Use
.BR setreuid (2):
.IP
.nf
setreuid(EUID, EUID)   \(-> sets RUID and EUID to oracle's uid
system("/bin/sh")      \(-> dash sees EUID == UID, no drop
.fi
.PP
Chaining two libc calls in ret2libc requires one extra trick:
.B system
expects its single argument at the top of the stack when it starts.
.B setreuid
takes two. Whichever you call first will leave its arguments on the
stack as garbage that the next call sees in place of its own arguments.
You need a tiny gadget --
.I "pop ; pop ; ret"
-- that pops setreuid's two arguments off the stack and then returns
into the next call. Such epilogues live throughout libc; any will do.
The full chain becomes:

.nf
[ filler to offset ]
[ &setreuid ][ &pop_pop_ret ][ uid ][ uid ]
[ &system ][ &exit ][ &"/bin/sh" ]
.fi

The reference exploit looks the
.B oracle
uid up at runtime via
.BR getpwnam (3).

.SS WHAT YOU NEED TO FIND

.TP
1.
The offset for
.IR vuln2 .
Recompute. It will not match
.IR vuln1 .
.TP
2.
The
.I file offset
of
.B system
inside
.IR libc.so.6 .
You're not after its loaded address (which moves with
.BR ASLR ),
you're after the unchanging position of the symbol within the library
file. The shell command:
.RS
.nf
.B "    LIBC=$(ldd /opt/lab/build/vuln2 | awk '/libc/ {print $3}')"
.B "    nm -D --defined-only \"$LIBC\" | awk '$3==\"system\" {print $1}'"
.fi
.RE
.TP
3.
The file offset of
.B exit
inside libc. Same approach as
.BR system .
.TP
4.
The file offset of the string
.B "/bin/sh"
inside libc. The shell command is:
.RS
.nf
.B "    strings -a -t x \"$LIBC\" | awk '/\\/bin\\/sh/ {print $1}'"
.fi
.RE
.TP
5.
The file offset of
.BR setreuid (2)
inside libc. Same approach as
.BR system .
.TP
6.
The file offset of any
.I "pop ; pop ; ret"
gadget inside libc.
.B objdump -d
on libc and grep its disassembly for sequential
.B pop
opcodes (0x58..0x5f) followed by
.B "ret"
(0xc3); pick any address that matches.
.TP
7.
The
.I runtime
address of
.B printf
in this process.
.B vuln2
prints it for you on its third leak line (after
.I &buffer
and
.IR &saved-eip ).
From that you can compute
.I libc base = printf_runtime - printf_file_offset
and from libc base, the runtime addresses of
.BR system ,
.BR exit ,
and the
.B "/bin/sh"
string.

.SS WHEN IT WORKS

.B vuln2
is setuid
.BR oracle .
Successful exploitation gives you a shell that can read
.IR /srv/flag2.txt .
Same submission shape as Phase Phi.

